--TEST--
A class claiming `Parses` owes the one member the contract requires, so a class with no `parse` is refused where it is declared and never at a binding site that names it — `rule:expressions/try-parse` and `rule:classes/comparable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/parses
// `Parses` is what a binding site asks a class for instead of naming one, so the
// question "may this class stand in a capture" has to be answerable from the
// class's own declaration and nowhere else. That makes the missing member an
// error *here*, at the class, rather than at the route that declared a capture at
// it -- one diagnostic naming the member, instead of one per site pointing back
// at a class that never said what it owed.
//
// `tryParse` is not the member being asked for: it is a default body on the
// interface, so a class inherits it and cannot discharge `parse` by writing it.
// One implementation of "is this text a `Slug`", never two.
class Slug implements Parses
{
    public tainted string $text = "";
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0449]: `Slug` does not declare `parse`, which `Parses` requires
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
